## Migration Analysis

Your code has **three** patterns requiring migration:

| API | Pattern | Replacement |
|-----|---------|-------------|
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Blocking call interruption | `CancellationToken.WaitHandle` |
| `Thread.Abort()` | Timeout enforcement | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |
| `ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` | Blocking wait that must be cancellable | `WaitHandle.WaitAny` with token |

---

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly ManualResetEvent _newEvent = new(false);
    private readonly Queue<string> _queue = new();
    private readonly object _lock = new();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private Thread? _thread;

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop);
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        var handles = new[] { _newEvent, _cts.Token.WaitHandle };

        while (!_cts.Token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // Blocks until either a new event arrives OR cancellation is requested
            int signaled = WaitHandle.WaitAny(handles);

            if (signaled == 1) // CancellationToken.WaitHandle fired
                break;

            _newEvent.Reset();

            string? item = null;
            lock (_lock)
            {
                if (_queue.Count > 0)
                    item = _queue.Dequeue();
            }

            if (item is not null)
                Handle(item);
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        lock (_lock) { _queue.Enqueue(item); }
        _newEvent.Set();
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts.Cancel();          // wakes WaitAny immediately
        _thread?.Join(5000);    // wait up to 5s for clean exit
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts.Dispose();
        _newEvent.Dispose();
    }
}
```

---

## Key Changes Explained

1. **`WaitHandle.WaitAny`** — replaces bare `WaitOne()`. The array includes both the event *and* `_cts.Token.WaitHandle`, so the thread wakes on either signal.

2. **`_cts.Cancel()` replaces both `Interrupt` and `Abort`** — a single cooperative signal cleanly exits the loop. No forced termination needed.

3. **Race condition fix** — the original code could throw on `Dequeue()` if multiple items were enqueued (only one `Set()` survives a `Reset()`). The migrated version guards with `_queue.Count > 0`.

4. **`IDisposable`** — `CancellationTokenSource` leaks timers/callbacks if not disposed.

---

## Pitfalls to Watch

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---------|---------------|
| Removing `Thread.Abort` without replacing the wake-up mechanism | `Join(5000)` alone won't unblock `WaitOne()` — you *must* signal via the token's WaitHandle |
| Not checking `_queue.Count` after wake | The event can be signaled spuriously or reset before dequeue — always guard |
| Forgetting to dispose `CancellationTokenSource` | Leaks internal kernel objects and registered callbacks |
| Assuming cancellation is instant | If `Handle(item)` is long-running, add `_cts.Token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` checkpoints inside it |

---

## Optional: Modern async alternative (Channel-based)

For new code on .NET 8, `Channel<T>` eliminates manual threading entirely:

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly Channel<string> _channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<string>();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private Task? _worker;

    public void Start() => _worker = Task.Run(RunLoopAsync);

    private async Task RunLoopAsync()
    {
        await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(_cts.Token))
        {
            Handle(item);
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item) => _channel.Writer.TryWrite(item);

    public async Task StopAsync()
    {
        _channel.Writer.Complete();
        await (_worker ?? Task.CompletedTask).WaitAsync(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
        _cts.Cancel();
    }

    public void Dispose() => _cts.Dispose();
}
```

This version is lock-free, backpressure-aware, and fully cancellation-integrated — but requires callers to be async-capable.
